WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.253
MARCUS DEMNERT: Ahoy, captain!

00:00:01.253 --> 00:00:02.295
What are you doing there?

00:00:02.295 --> 00:00:03.253
FREDRIK GORANSSON: Arr!

00:00:03.253 --> 00:00:05.610
I'm trying to build a Docker
image for this old Java

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:07.060
application that I have.

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:09.780
It's a great application that
keeps track of all my fishing

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:10.393
routes.

00:00:10.393 --> 00:00:12.060
Let me tell you about
those, by the way.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:13.860
MARCUS DEMNERT: Trim
your sails, Captain.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:15.212
Why don't you use Jib for that?

00:00:15.212 --> 00:00:16.170
FREDRIK GORANSSON: Jib?

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:16.970
Jib?

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:18.670
I know all about sails--

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:21.060
genoas, spinnakers, topsails!

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.060
MARCUS DEMNERT:
Hang on, Captain.

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:25.950
Jib is a tool that builds
containers for your job

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:28.770
applications without needing
much knowledge of Docker

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:29.940
best practices.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:31.530
FREDRIK GORANSSON:
Shiver me timbers!

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:34.596
Tell me more about these
newfangled trickery.

00:00:34.596 --> 00:00:38.082
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:40.053 --> 00:00:41.970
MARCUS DEMNERT: Take me
to your helm, Captain.

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:43.680
FREDRIK GORANSSON:
You mean my terminal?

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.330
MARCUS DEMNERT: Yeah, right.

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:47.640
Let's start with the
basic "Ahoy, world!"

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:48.657
Java application.

00:00:48.657 --> 00:00:51.240
FREDRIK GORANSSON: Oh, you mean
a "Hello, world!" application.

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:52.680
MARCUS DEMNERT: Yeah.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:55.648
Let's go and grab a sample
application from the web.

00:00:55.648 --> 00:00:57.440
FREDRIK GORANSSON: Oh,
I know just the one.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:58.840
When do we comb this?

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:01.710
MARCUS DEMNERT: So Jib
works great with both Gradle

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:02.670
and Maven.

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:04.970
I'll show you how to do
it with Gradle today.

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:05.513
OK, Captain?

00:01:05.513 --> 00:01:07.680
FREDRIK GORANSSON: Oh, you
run a tight ship, sailor.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:08.443
Let's do that.

00:01:08.443 --> 00:01:10.110
MARCUS DEMNERT: First,
let's take a look

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.180
at the Jib page online.

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:15.020
The Gradle quick start shows
you how to get started.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:17.070
Super easy-- just
add the plug-in

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:18.850
to your build.gradle file.

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:21.270
FREDRIK GORANSSON: Arr, but
where should I put that code?

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:26.010
MARCUS DEMNERT: Put it directly
after that build script entry.

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:27.540
That's all you need.

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:30.270
FREDRIK GORANSSON: Well,
that's smooth sailing, sailor.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:32.100
MARCUS DEMNERT: Now,
let's build that.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.460
Just type "gradle jib".

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:35.460
FREDRIK GORANSSON: Narr!

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:36.390
Pirates!

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:38.340
That did not work, sailor.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:40.920
MARCUS DEMNERT: I forgot
to say, not only does Jib

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:42.730
build the container image.

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:45.502
It also sends it to a container
registry of your choice.

00:01:45.502 --> 00:01:47.460
FREDRIK GORANSSON: Oh,
I keep all my containers

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:48.930
on the ship, sailor--

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:51.617
I mean Google Cloud
Container registry.

00:01:51.617 --> 00:01:52.950
MARCUS DEMNERT: OK, that's good.

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:57.610
Now, just add the image
argument to the same command.

00:01:57.610 --> 00:01:59.620
FREDRIK GORANSSON:
Well, that was easy.

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:01.370
MARCUS DEMNERT: That's
all there is to it.

00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:03.900
FREDRIK GORANSSON: Ship shape
and Bristol fashion, sailor.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:06.448
MARCUS DEMNERT: Let's try
it out locally with Docker.

00:02:06.448 --> 00:02:08.115
FREDRIK GORANSSON:
Oh, I know this one--

00:02:08.115 --> 00:02:10.030
"docker run".

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:12.660
Now, let's shake
as in shipshape.

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:15.270
I'm going to go to the localhost
and check that the app is

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:17.400
running through Docker.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.310
MARCUS DEMNERT: So now that
you have your container image,

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:22.650
you can run it anywhere
you can run containers,

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:23.814
like Kubernetes.

00:02:23.814 --> 00:02:25.080
FREDRIK GORANSSON: Kubernetes?

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:28.050
Did you know that Kubernetes
means "helmsman" in Greek?

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:29.970
Now, that's another
funny nautical reference

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:31.740
we put in this script.

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:33.420
MARCUS DEMNERT: Thanks, Captain.

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:36.490
Anyway, that's how easy Jib
makes it to containerize

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:37.830
your Java application.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:39.272
No Docker mastery needed.

00:02:39.272 --> 00:02:40.230
FREDRIK GORANSSON: Arr.

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:42.780
Now, don't forget to subscribe
for more great Google Cloud

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:44.130
Platform content.

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:47.480
[MUSIC PLAYING]

